`]})]})}function st(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(q,{...n})}):q(n)}const it=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:st,frontmatter:he},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),le={title:"Does a Bigger Turbo Really Mean More Lag? We Sized Five and Timed Them",date:"2026-09-11",updated:"2026-10-07",excerpt:"Five turbo wheels, 60 to 110 mm, on one 3.0 L six at the same 1.92 bar target. We timed each to boost at four speeds and found where lag turns into never.",readingMinutes:22,author:"Engine Simulator Team",draft:!1,image:"/blog/turbo-lag-cover.jpg",imageAlt:"A small turbocharger cartridge beside a much larger one on a steel workbench, both compressor wheels facing the camera and catching warm workshop light"};function E(n){const t={a:"a",blockquote:"blockquote",code:"code",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",img:"img",li:"li",ol:"ol",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Ask a forum whether a bigger turbo means more lag and you get a yes, a story about a friend's build, and no numbers. Nobody in the thread has timed it, because timing it on a real engine means five turbochargers, five manifold fabrications and a dyno day for each."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In our simulator the compressor wheel is a slider. Load the turbocharged 3.0 L inline-six, hold it at 3,500 rpm on the dyno and snap the throttle open. With the stock 82 mm wheel the manifold crosses half a bar of boost at 0.33 s. Drag the slider to 60 mm and the same crossing comes at 0.10 s. Drag it to 110 mm and you can wait the full eight seconds of the test: the manifold settles at +0.22 bar and never reaches half a bar. On this engine, lag is a delay at the small end of the slider and a refusal at the big end."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Bigger means more lag at every speed we tested, and the penalty outruns the size change. At 3,500 rpm, time to ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"+0.5 bar"})," of boost runs ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.10 / 0.17 / 0.33 / 0.82 s"})," for 60, 70, 82 and 95 mm wheels, and the 110 mm wheel never gets there. Time to the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"1.92 bar target"}),": ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.12 / 0.21 / 0.42 / 1.36 s"}),", then never."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Rotor inertia rises ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"×20.7"})," from 60 to 110 mm, following the fifth power of diameter. Turbine throat area rises ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"×3.4"}),", the square. The slowest wheel is also the freest-breathing one: 2.02 bar of exhaust backpressure at 7,000 rpm against 5.14 for the 60 mm."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Below 5,000 rpm the 110 mm wheel never holds half a bar, and it holds the 1.92 bar target only from 5,000 rpm up. Torque at 2,500 rpm: ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"512.1 N·m"})," on the 60 mm wheel, ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"247.0"})," on the 110."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["The 70 mm wheel reads the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"most"})," peak power, 429.63 hp against 385.48 stock, because its wastegate is sized off the wheel, runs out of travel and lets the manifold creep to 2.61 bar. The simulator charges for the backpressure that comes with it, which is why the 60 mm wheel, creeping to 2.78 bar with up to 5.14 bar of exhaust behind it, peaks at 5,060 rpm and falls behind at the top. It does not charge for heat. Treat the small wheels as the fast end of the lag curve."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Two 59.5 mm wheels spool ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"slower"})," than one 75.0 mm wheel in this model, 1.15 s against 0.86 s to 90% at 2,500 rpm, for the same power to about 1%. The real twin-turbo advantage lives in manifold packaging the model does not have."]}),`
`]}),`
