`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"The shape is right."})," Efficiency rises with compression ratio, with diminishing returns per additional ratio point, exactly as the ideal relation predicts."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"The magnitude is not."})," The ideal cycle assumes no heat loss, no friction, no gas-exchange work and instantaneous combustion — none of which a real (or realistically simulated) engine gets. A measured brake efficiency in the low thirties for a petrol engine is realistic; the gap between it and 56%+ is the honest cost of every assumption the ideal cycle waives away."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"The real curve turns over. The ideal curve never does."})," That is not noise — it is a second, independent physical limit intruding on the first. Experiment 2 measures it directly."]}),`
