`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A two-stroke fires every crank revolution instead of every other one, so the naive prediction is double the power at equal displacement. The measured ratio here is higher than 2x, not lower — the full mechanism (why it is not exactly 2x on every engine, and where the real losses live) is the subject of ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/two-stroke-vs-four-stroke",children:"a dedicated two-stroke versus four-stroke measurement"})," built on the same method. A reader-reproducible version needing no spec editing: the shipped ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=ts_single_250",children:"249 cc two-stroke single"})," against the shipped ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=single_thumper_500",children:"499 cc four-stroke single"})," — half the displacement, 62% more power, and peak torque within 1.5% of each other."]}),`
