`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Splitting the same swept volume eight ways instead of two buys ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"1.0%"})," more peak power. Cylinder count is not, at fixed displacement and fixed redline, a meaningful power lever in this measurement. What a class should be pushed to ask next is exactly what this experiment deliberately held fixed: the redline. In a real engine, more and smaller cylinders means lighter reciprocating parts, which is what actually lets a real high-cylinder-count engine turn faster and make more power — a mechanism this controlled comparison does not include on purpose, precisely so it can isolate the one variable it claims to isolate."]}),`
