`]})]})}function mt(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(I,{...n})}):I(n)}const gt=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:mt,frontmatter:pe},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),me={title:"The 4 Strokes of an Engine: Intake, Compression, Power, Exhaust",date:"2026-06-11",updated:"2026-08-22",excerpt:"What happens in each of the four strokes of an engine (intake, compression, power, exhaust), why only one of them makes power, and an engine you can watch run.",readingMinutes:8,draft:!1,image:"/blog/four-stroke-cycle.svg",imageAlt:"Diagram of the four strokes of an engine cycle: intake with the piston falling, compression with both valves closed, power with the burning charge driving the piston down, and exhaust with the spent gas pushed out"};function M(n){const t={a:"a",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",li:"li",ol:"ol",p:"p",strong:"strong",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Open the hood of almost any car built in the last hundred years and you find the same machine: a four-stroke piston engine. The fuel changed, the electronics changed, the materials changed. The cycle did not. Nikolaus Otto got it right in 1876."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The idea fits in one sentence: trap an air-fuel mixture in a cylinder, squeeze it, ignite it, and let the burn shove a piston that turns a crank. Most explanations go fuzzy in the detail, so this one takes the cycle stroke by stroke."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: What Are the Four Strokes?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["One full cycle takes ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"two crankshaft revolutions (720°)"})," and four piston strokes:"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Intake"}),": piston moves down, intake valve open, cylinder fills with air and fuel."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Compression"}),": both valves closed, piston moves up, mixture squeezed to a tenth of its volume."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Power"}),": spark ignites the mixture, pressure drives the piston down. This is the only stroke that produces work."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Exhaust"}),": piston moves up, exhaust valve open, burned gas pushed out."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Then the cycle repeats. At 6,000 RPM, each cylinder runs the full sequence ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"50 times per second"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Stroke 1: Intake"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'The piston descends from top dead center with the intake valve open. The growing cylinder volume drops the pressure, and atmospheric pressure pushes air in through the intake tract. The engine does not "suck" so much as the atmosphere shoves.'}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The amount of air that gets in is the single biggest factor in how much power the engine can make. The throttle plate is a restriction in this path: close it and the cylinder fills less, burns less fuel, makes less torque. At wide open throttle the restriction is gone and the engine breathes freely."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Stroke 2: Compression"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Both valves close and the piston rises, squeezing the mixture into the small volume above the piston. Gasoline engines run a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"compression ratio"})," of 10:1 to 13:1."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compression earns its keep through thermodynamics: the more you squeeze the charge before burning it, the more work you can extract from the expansion afterwards. Higher compression ratio means higher efficiency, up to the point where the heat of compression ignites the fuel before the spark does. That uncontrolled ignition is ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/what-is-engine-knock",children:"engine knock"})}),", destructive enough that we wrote a separate post on what causes it and how to stop it."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Diesel engines exploit this on purpose: at 16:1 to 23:1 compression, the air alone gets hot enough (over 800 K) that fuel injected into it ignites spontaneously. No spark plug needed."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Stroke 3: Power"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A few degrees before the piston reaches the top, the spark plug fires. The flame front takes a few milliseconds to sweep across the chamber, so ignition has to come ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"early"}),": the goal is peak pressure arriving a few degrees after top dead center, when the crank geometry can best use it."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Pressure spikes to 50–100 bar and drives the piston down. The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"connecting rod"})," turns that linear shove into crankshaft rotation, the crank-slider mechanism at the heart of every piston engine built."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In a four-cylinder engine at idle, each cylinder fires once per 720°, so the crank gets four discrete kicks per two revolutions. The flywheel smooths those kicks into continuous rotation."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Stroke 4: Exhaust"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exhaust valve opens a few degrees before bottom dead center, while there is still useful pressure to start gas moving, and the rising piston pushes the burned charge out. At the top, for a few degrees, ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"both"})," valves are open. This ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"valve overlap"})," uses the momentum of outflowing exhaust to help pull in fresh charge. Race cams stretch the overlap for top-end power at the cost of a lumpy idle."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Then the intake valve opens fully, the piston descends, and the engine is back at stroke one."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Parts Doing the Work"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Eight components carry the whole cycle:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Piston"}),": the moving wall that compresses the charge and receives the power stroke."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Piston rings"}),": seal combustion pressure above the piston and scrape oil below it."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Connecting rod"}),": links piston to crank, converting linear shove into rotation."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Crankshaft"}),": the rotating output; its throws set the stroke length."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Camshaft"}),": geared to spin at half crank speed (720° per cycle), opening each valve at the right stroke."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Valves"}),": intake lets charge in, exhaust lets burned gas out; both seal shut for compression and power."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Spark plug"}),": fires once per cycle, a few degrees before top dead center."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Flywheel"}),": stores rotational energy to carry the crank through the three non-power strokes."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"From Explosions to Horsepower"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Two terms get conflated constantly:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Torque"})," is twist: how hard each power stroke shoves the crank. The amount of mixture the cylinder burns per cycle sets it."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Power"})," is torque times speed: how ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"often"})," those shoves happen. In imperial units, ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/tools/horsepower-calculator",children:"horsepower equals torque (lb-ft) times RPM divided by 5,252"}),"."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That formula explains real dyno curves. Torque peaks where the engine breathes best (its volumetric-efficiency sweet spot); power keeps climbing past it because rising RPM outweighs slowly falling torque, until breathing collapses and both fall. You can sweep a virtual engine on a dyno and watch this happen in our free ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator",children:"engine simulator"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why More Cylinders? And Why Does a V8 Sound Like That?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"One cylinder gives one bang per 720°. Eight cylinders, fired at even intervals, give a bang every 90°: smoother torque, a smaller flywheel, and a higher exhaust pulse frequency."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The sound of an engine is mostly ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"firing frequency"}),". A four-cylinder at 6,000 RPM fires 200 times a second. A crossplane V8 produces its signature burble because its firing intervals are uneven ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"per bank"}),", layering pulse spacings the ear reads as a low, syncopated rumble. Same physics and a different firing order give a different character."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Watch the Whole Cycle Run"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Diagrams freeze the engine; the real thing is a blur of valves and flame arriving 50 times a second. In our in-browser ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator",children:"engine simulator"})," you can crank an inline-four or a V8, watch the pistons and combustion events in 3D, pull a dyno curve, and read an event log that narrates each state change in plain language: starter engagement, first fire, rev limiter, even fuel starvation from ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/what-is-vapor-lock",children:"vapor lock"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Four-Stroke Engine FAQs"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why is it called the Otto cycle?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["After Nikolaus Otto, who built the first practical compressed-charge four-stroke in 1876. The thermodynamic idealization of his engine (isentropic compression, constant-volume heat addition, isentropic expansion) still carries his name. The full derivation of why compression ratio drives efficiency, and where knock sets the ceiling, is in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/otto-cycle-explained",children:"The Otto Cycle Explained"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"What's the difference between a four-stroke and a two-stroke?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A two-stroke fires every revolution instead of every other one, using ports and crankcase pressure instead of valves. It makes more power per displacement, burns dirtier, and drinks more fuel, which is why two-strokes survive mainly in chainsaws, dirt bikes, and giant ship engines."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"What limits an engine's redline?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Mostly valvetrain control and piston acceleration. Past a certain speed the valves "float" (springs cannot close them fast enough) and connecting-rod stress grows with the square of RPM. The rev limiter cuts spark or fuel below that point. Hit it in the simulator and the event log names the cut.'}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why does an engine need to idle? Why not just stop?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A four-stroke produces no power below self-sustaining speed; it needs stored rotational momentum to carry the crank through the three non-power strokes. Drop below idle speed without enough torque and the cycle collapses into a stall. Start-stop systems avoid idling by using a beefed-up starter to relaunch the cycle on demand."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is a jet engine also a four-stroke?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["No. A jet runs the same conceptual steps (intake, compression, combustion, exhaust) ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"continuously and simultaneously"})," in different parts of the machine, rather than sequentially in one cylinder. We compare the two approaches in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/jet-engine-vs-rocket-engine",children:"Jet Engine vs Rocket Engine"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Related Reading"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/otto-cycle-explained",children:"Otto Cycle Explained: Live P-V Diagram and Efficiency Formula"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/what-is-engine-knock",children:"What Is Engine Knock? Causes, Sounds, and How to Stop It"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-turbocharger-works",children:"How a Turbocharger Works (Boost, Lag, and Wastegates)"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/what-is-vapor-lock",children:"What Is Vapor Lock? Symptoms, Causes and How to Fix It"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/jet-engine-vs-rocket-engine",children:"Jet Engine vs Rocket Engine: Which Is More Powerful?"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/ai-in-engines",children:"AI in Engines: Can ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini Run One?"})}),`
`]})]})}function ft(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(M,{...n})}):M(n)}const xt=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:ft,frontmatter:me},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ge={title:"How Does a Jet Engine Start? Step by Step",date:"2026-07-28",updated:"2026-08-22",excerpt:"A jet engine starts by spinning its compressor, adding ignition and fuel, then accelerating to governed idle. The sequence, the gauges, and the failure modes.",readingMinutes:8,draft:!1,image:"/blog/jet-engine-start-sequence.png",imageAlt:"Instrument-style graph showing compressor speed and exhaust temperature through motoring, light-off, self-acceleration, starter cutout, and idle"};function _(n){const t={a:"a",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",li:"li",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A jet engine can pull an aircraft through the sky, but at rest it cannot start itself. The stationary compressor provides almost no airflow; without compressed air, fuel cannot burn stably; without combustion, the turbine cannot drive the compressor. Starting breaks this loop by handing the work from an outside power source to the turbine."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: How Does a Jet Engine Start?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A starter spins the compressor to establish airflow. The operator or controller selects ignition, then introduces fuel once the engine-specific cranking condition is met. Light-off, the first successful combustion, raises exhaust temperature and adds turbine power. The engine then passes self-sustaining speed; the starter normally keeps assisting until its specified later cutout speed, and the engine continues to governed idle."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Ordering matters: fuel without enough airflow or ignition can collect unburned; too much fuel for the airflow can overheat the turbine; a weak starter may produce light-off but not idle. The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-32b-chapter-5-engine-starting-systems",children:"FAA's current Aviation Maintenance Technician Handbook—Powerplant, Chapter 5"})," says the starter must provide initial airflow and help the engine reach self-sustaining speed."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why Does a Jet Engine Need a Starter?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Once running, a gas turbine closes its own power loop: compressor pressure, combustion heat, and expanding gas turn a turbine connected back to the compressor. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/BGP/compress.html",children:"NASA Glenn's compressor explainer"})," shows why every turbine engine must raise inlet-air pressure before the combustor."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"At zero RPM, the turbine has no hot gas to extract work from while the compressor still needs torque. An electric starter or starter-generator may use batteries or electrical ground power. An air-turbine starter uses compressed air from an auxiliary power unit (APU), a pneumatic ground-start cart, or cross-bleed from a running engine. Each method creates compressor airflow for controlled light-off."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The APU usually solves the same smaller-scale problem with a battery-powered electric starter. Once running, it can supply electrical and pneumatic energy to start a main engine."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The source, cranking speed, starter limit, and switch sequence are aircraft-specific. This post explains the physics and is not an operating procedure; use an approved checklist and trained personnel on real hardware."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Six-Step Jet Engine Start Sequence"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"1. The Starter Gets an Energy Source"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Electrical power may drive a small engine directly; larger engines often use compressed air to spin an air-turbine starter geared to the core. A slow spool produces weak airflow, so the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/16_afh_ch15.pdf",children:"FAA Airplane Flying Handbook's turbine transition chapter"})," emphasizes adequate start power and close monitoring of temperature and acceleration."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"2. The Compressor Begins to Turn"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The starter rotates the compressor before fuel burns, moving air through the combustor and turbine. On a two-spool turbofan it normally turns the high-pressure core, commonly indicated as N2; fan speed, N1, responds differently. A single-spool turbojet has one compressor-turbine assembly. Pre-light-off cranking establishes airflow; after light-off, continued starter assistance increases airflow for stable burning and cooling."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"3. Ignition Is Armed"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"High-energy igniters provide a spark when an ignitable mixture reaches the combustor. Some systems sequence them automatically; others require a separate selection. Some engines use continuous ignition in particular conditions. The logic is type-specific."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"4. Fuel Enters and Light-Off Occurs"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["At the specified cranking condition, fuel enters and the spark establishes a continuous flame. NASA's ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/BGP/burner.html",children:"combustor overview"})," places this between compressor and turbine: high-pressure air mixes with fuel and creates hot gas to turn the turbine. A prompt EGT, ITT, or equivalent temperature rise normally confirms light-off, but the starter must still support acceleration."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"5. Starter and Turbine Accelerate Together"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Combustion raises turbine torque and core speed. Growing airflow lets the fuel control add fuel while managing temperature and compressor stability. The operator or controller watches speed, temperature, fuel flow, and oil pressure. A flame without continued acceleration is not a successful start."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"6. The Starter Cuts Out and the Engine Reaches Idle"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The turbine first reaches a speed where it can continue accelerating without being driven solely by the starter. The starter normally remains engaged to an engine-specific cutout point above that milestone, then disengages while the engine continues to governed idle. Temperature often eases as airflow grows. A complete start requires all indications to be normal against manufacturer-defined limits."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"What Gauges Matter During a Jet Start?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Names differ, but these indications tell the story:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Indication"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"What it reveals during start"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Core speed (N2, Ng, or N on many engines)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Confirms starter rotation, minimum fuel-on speed, continuing acceleration, and arrival at idle"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fan/low-spool speed (N1, where fitted)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Shows low-spool response; it is not interchangeable with N2 on a multi-spool engine"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"EGT, ITT, TIT, or TOT"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Confirms light-off and exposes an over-temperature trend"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fuel flow"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Confirms metering and helps explain a missing, slow, or excessively hot light-off"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Oil pressure"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Confirms that lubrication pressure develops as the engine turns"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Starter or pneumatic indication"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Confirms starter engagement, available start energy, and eventual cutout"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/phak/chapter-7-aircraft-systems",children:"FAA Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, Chapter 7"})," covers turbine construction, instruments, and hot/hung starts. The aircraft manual defines what each gauge must show and when."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"What Can Go Wrong During Start?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Abnormal start"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"What it means"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"What the instruments tend to show"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"No light-off"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"The intended flame never establishes after fuel and ignition are selected"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fuel may be indicated, but there is no expected temperature rise or combustion-driven acceleration"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Wet start"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fuel enters without successful ignition and unburned fuel accumulates in or downstream of the combustor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"No normal light-off; evidence depends on the engine and installation"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Hot start"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Temperature exceeds the engine's start limit because heat release is too high for the available airflow"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fast EGT/ITT/TOT rise, often with inadequate spool acceleration"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Hung start"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"The engine lights but acceleration stagnates below self-sustaining idle"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Temperature and fuel flow may be present while core speed stops increasing normally"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A no-light-off can become a wet start if fuel continues entering. A rapid approach to the temperature limit is an impending-hot-start cue; an actual hot start exceeds that engine's defined start limit. The FAA describes hung or false starts as failures to accelerate properly to idle, with insufficient starting power among the possible causes."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"These are recognition cues, not troubleshooting instructions. Cutoff, motoring, cooldown, inspection, and restart actions vary; follow the specific AFM/POH and engine manual."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"FADEC vs Hydromechanical Starting"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A hydromechanical control schedules fuel with pumps, governors, valves, and pressure signals. The mechanisms and signals designed into it bound its protection, which leaves the crew more responsibility on some installations."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A FADEC reads speed, temperature, pressure, lever position, and other inputs, then commands fuel and may coordinate ignition and starter logic. It can limit start fuel or react to abnormal trends. It reduces workload without making every start identical or fault-proof."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Our two J85 presets are a teaching comparison, not two certified real-world J85 installations. They share the same simulated gas path. The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"hydromechanical"})," preset exposes more of the raw fuel-scheduling risk, while the fictional ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"FADEC retrofit"})," adds digital limiting and hot-start protection."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Try the Start Sequence Safely in the Simulator"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Open the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=jet_j85",children:"hydromechanical J85"}),". For a clean simulated run, press ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"STOP"})," if the spool is turning, set the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"POWER LEVER (PLA)"})," to 0%, select ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"GOV"}),", confirm the fuel cock is closed, and wait for N to stop. If you previously edited the preset, switch to another engine and back first."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Press ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"START"}),"; it engages the simulated starter and igniters. Watch N rise as the compressor creates airflow. At ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"13% N or higher"}),", open the fuel cock and watch for light-off: EGT rises, the spool accelerates, and the event log narrates the handoff. The modeled starter torque ends at ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"38% N"}),", and the governed idle target is ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"46% N"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Then press ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"STOP"})," and repeat with the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=jet_j85_fadec",children:"FADEC retrofit J85"}),". Its normal-start milestones are the same, but its digital control meters and protects fuel differently. Slow the time scale to 0.25× if you want to study the gauges."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Those 13%, 38%, and 46% figures are ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"simulator-preset settings only"}),", not J85 instructions or transferable limits. The simulator is the safe place to explore its virtual fault controls without risking hardware; results depend on the exact starting state and control settings."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Jet Engine Starting FAQs"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"What is light-off in a jet engine?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Light-off is the first successful burn in the combustor. Temperature rise confirms combustion, but a complete start requires continued acceleration through self-sustaining speed to governed idle."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why not add fuel as soon as the starter turns?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Very low compressor speed may provide too little airflow to stabilize flame and cool the turbine. Each engine therefore has a defined fuel-introduction condition."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Do jet-engine igniters run all the time?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Not necessarily. They start the flame and may be selected continuously in certain conditions. Established jet combustion is continuous and needs no spark per cycle."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"What spins first on a turbofan?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The starter commonly drives the high-pressure core, making N2 the primary start indication. Fan N1 responds through gas-path coupling. Single-spool engines, including the simulated J85, lack that distinction."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Can a jet engine restart without a starter?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Some engines can windmill within an approved in-flight restart envelope; others need starter assistance. The envelope and procedure are aircraft-specific."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is a hot start the same as a compressor stall?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["No. A hot start is a start-temperature problem, usually involving too much heat release for the available airflow. A ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/compressor-stall-vs-surge",children:"compressor stall or surge"})," is an aerodynamic breakdown of compressor flow. A poor start can involve both, but they describe different failures."]}),`
