`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A torque converter multiplies engine torque at low speed ratios by roughly ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"2.0–2.3x"})," — measured here at ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"2.10x"})," at a dead stop on a 5.0 V8 — and the multiplication falls to 1.00x at a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"coupling point"})," around 85% speed ratio."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Torque multiplication does not create power: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"TR(SR)·SR ≤ 1"})," everywhere the model runs. What the stator gives back in torque, it takes from the turbine's speed."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Measured on four real engines, the automatic reached 100 km/h before the manual on every single one"})," — by 0.60 seconds on a 5.0 V8, by 1.58 seconds on a 2.0 four."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"More gears bought very little."})," A real 4-speed, 8-speed and 10-speed on the same car and engine landed within 0.21 seconds of each other to 100 km/h; the 10-speed was quickest by a hair. At a steady 100 km/h the 4-speed and the 10-speed sat 915 rpm apart."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A lockup clutch removes the one loss a converter has left once it is coupled: once engaged, slip collapses to a few rpm and the converter is no longer doing any work at all."}),`
