`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"On this page:"})," ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#quick-answer-how-does-a-torque-converter-work",children:"Quick answer"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-three-parts-and-what-each-one-does",children:"The three parts"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#what-multiplication-costs",children:"What multiplication costs"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#a-measured-launch-second-by-second",children:"A measured launch"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#automatic-vs-manual-which-is-actually-quicker",children:"Automatic vs manual"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#does-adding-gears-actually-make-a-car-quicker",children:"Does adding gears help"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-lockup-clutch",children:"The lockup clutch"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#stall-speed-the-number-on-the-spec-sheet",children:"Stall speed"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-the-car-creeps-forward-in-drive",children:"Creep"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#try-it-in-the-simulator",children:"Try it in the simulator"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#frequently-asked-questions",children:"FAQ"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-these-numbers-were-made",children:"How these numbers were made"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: How Does a Torque Converter Work?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A torque converter connects the engine to the gearbox through spinning fluid rather than a friction plate. Three vaned wheels sit face to face inside a sealed housing. An ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"impeller"})," on the engine flings fluid outward; a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"turbine"})," on the gearbox catches it; and a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"stator"})," between them, held still by a one-way clutch at low speed, redirects the returning fluid so it adds to the impeller's push instead of fighting it. That redirection is where the torque multiplication comes from. As the turbine catches up to the impeller's speed, the stator has nothing left to redirect, freewheels, and the converter becomes a plain fluid coupling passing 1:1 torque with a few percent of slip."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The rest of this page is a measured account of what that means for a real launch, and a controlled comparison against a manual clutch on the same car."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Three Parts, and What Each One Does"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Part"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Attached to"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Job"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Impeller"})," (pump)"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Engine crankshaft"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Spins fluid outward by centrifugal force, launching it toward the turbine"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Turbine"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Gearbox input shaft"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Catches the fluid, is pushed round by its momentum"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Stator"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"A one-way clutch, fixed to the housing at low speed ratio"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Redirects fluid returning from the turbine so it re-enters the impeller helping, not fighting"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["At a dead stop the impeller is spinning fast and the turbine is barely moving — engine speed is high, driveshaft speed is near zero. The fluid returning from the turbine hits the stator at a sharp angle, and without the stator it would slam into the back of the impeller's vanes and cancel out most of the flow. The stator's ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/7617677",children:"one-way clutch"})," holds it still against that flow and redirects it, so the returning fluid adds energy back into the impeller's circuit instead of subtracting it. That redirection is the entire mechanism, and it is why a converter can put more torque at the turbine than the engine is putting into the impeller: the difference is fluid momentum the stator caught and threw back in, not energy from nowhere."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Two numbers describe where in that cycle the converter is at any instant:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Speed ratio (SR)"})," = turbine speed ÷ pump speed. Zero at a dead stop, approaching 1.0 (never quite reaching it) once the car is at speed."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Torque ratio (TR)"})," = torque delivered at the turbine ÷ torque absorbed at the pump. Highest at SR = 0 (",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"stall"}),"), falling in a roughly straight line to 1.00 at the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"coupling point"})," — typically somewhere around SR 0.85–0.90, and ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.sonnax.com/tech_resources/86-high-performance-converters-stall-speed-core-selection-and-more",children:"industry stall torque ratios published for production converters typically run 1.5–2.5"})," — above which the stator freewheels and stops doing anything."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"What Multiplication Costs"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The stator's redirection is what multiplies torque, and it is paid for entirely in speed, not created from nothing. The simulator's converter module states the bound plainly:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['"Torque multiplication does not create power... Power out never exceeds power in iff ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"TR(SR)·SR ≤ 1"}),' everywhere."']}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Every gearbox measured for this article clears that bound with room to spare — the tightest, a 6.0 V12's converter, peaks the product at 0.86 against a ceiling of 1.0. It is why the model clamps the stall torque ratio at ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"2.8"})," rather than letting a slider push it further: above roughly 3.0 at these coupling ratios, the linear torque-ratio curve would need to deliver more power at the turbine than the pump absorbs, which no real fluid coupling can do."]}),`
