`]})]})}function kt(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(P,{...n})}):P(n)}const vt=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:kt,frontmatter:xe},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),je={title:"How Does a Rotary Engine Work? The Wankel Cycle, Measured",date:"2026-11-05",excerpt:"One triangular rotor, three chambers, one power pulse per rotor per shaft turn. How a Wankel works, why it burns oil, and how its torque compares to a piston.",readingMinutes:13,author:"Engine Simulator Team",draft:!1,image:"/blog/how-does-a-rotary-engine-work.png",imageAlt:"Cutaway of a Wankel rotary engine showing a triangular rotor inside its two-lobe housing, with the eccentric shaft at the centre and three working chambers between the rotor faces and the housing wall"};function O(n){const t={a:"a",blockquote:"blockquote",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",img:"img",li:"li",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A rotary engine has no pistons, no connecting rods and no poppet valves. A triangular rotor turns inside a two-lobed housing with its three tips sealing against the wall, and the three spaces between rotor and wall do the work of cylinders. Each space grows and shrinks as the rotor turns, and mixture, spark and exhaust pass through it in order."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That shape gives the Wankel its defining piece of arithmetic: one power pulse per rotor per turn of the shaft. This article explains the mechanism, then tests what it buys. At the same two pulses per shaft turn and 4,000 rpm, the simulator's ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_2r_1L3",children:"1.3 L two-rotor"})," swings between 18 and 445 N·m over a turn, while the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=i4_2L",children:"2.0 L inline-four"})," swings between −153 and 675 N·m. The story of why the engine left the market and returned is in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/rotary-engine-comeback",children:"why the rotary engine is coming back"}),"; this post covers the mechanism."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A Wankel is one triangular rotor in a two-lobed housing, forming three chambers. Each chamber takes in, compresses, burns and exhausts a charge as it travels round."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The output shaft turns three times per rotor revolution, with three power pulses in that revolution. That works out to one pulse per rotor per shaft turn, against one per two turns for a four-stroke cylinder."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["At equal pulses per turn the rotary's torque is far smoother in our model: peak torque is ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"2.31×"})," the mean on the two-rotor against ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"3.75×"})," on the inline-four. Swapping the engines' burns barely moves that, which points to stroke length, 270° of shaft per process against 180°. The finding covers torque only; the model has no vibration output."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Porting changes the idle far more than the power in the model: side port to peripheral lifts peak power 2.3% while complete idle burns fall from 100% to 41%."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Oil burn, apex seal wear, emissions and quench come from published sources here. The simulator models none of them."}),`
