`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"On this page:"})," ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#quick-answer-how-does-a-rotary-engine-work",children:"Quick answer"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#what-is-a-rotary-engine",children:"What is a rotary engine"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-the-three-chambers-share-one-rotor",children:"The three chambers"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#rotary-engine-vs-piston-engine",children:"Rotary vs piston"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-porting-changes-a-rotary-engine",children:"Porting"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-do-rotary-engines-burn-oil-and-wear-apex-seals",children:"Oil and apex seals"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#try-it-in-the-simulator",children:"Try it in the simulator"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#frequently-asked-questions",children:"FAQ"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-these-numbers-were-made",children:"How these numbers were made"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: How Does a Rotary Engine Work"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A rotary engine turns burning fuel into shaft rotation with a spinning rotor in place of pistons. The rotor is a rounded triangle riding on an eccentric lobe of the output shaft, inside a housing whose ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://patents.google.com/patent/US8985085B2/en",children:"epitrochoid shape defines two lobes"}),". Its tips stay against the wall, which divides the housing into three working chambers. As the rotor turns, each chamber swells to draw in mixture, shrinks to compress it, takes a spark near its smallest volume, swells again under combustion pressure, and shrinks to push the exhaust out through a port."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The rotor's rotation ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://dieselnet.com/tech/engine_alternatives.php",children:"completes all phases of the combustion cycle"}),", and the three chambers run that sequence a third of a rotor turn apart, so one fills while another fires. DieselNet's engineering summary gives the two numbers that matter: ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://dieselnet.com/tech/engine_alternatives.php",children:"the output shaft turns three times for each revolution of the rotor, and there are three power pulses per rotor revolution"}),". Dividing one by the other gives one power pulse per rotor per shaft turn. That last step is our arithmetic on the sourced figures. A four-stroke cylinder fires once every two shaft turns, so each rotor pulses twice as often as a cylinder."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"What Is a Rotary Engine"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'In a car, "rotary engine" means the Wankel: an internal combustion engine whose main moving parts turn in one direction and never reverse. It uses ports in the housing where a four-stroke uses poppet valves, and the rotor itself opens and closes them as it passes.'}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The design ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.audi.com/en/press-releases/sporty-beautiful-and-innovative-the-nsuwankel-spider-15608",children:"first ran at NSU on February 1, 1957"}),", and the NSU Spider became the first production car with a single-rotor Wankel. Mazda ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://uk.mazda-press.com/news/2020/mazda-motor-corporation-1920-2020----a-century-of-defying-convention/",children:"signed its licence with NSU in 1961"})," and carried the engine furthest. Its ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.mazda.com/en/about/history/greatcar/cosmo-sport/",children:"Cosmo Sport launched in May 1967"})," as the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/en/publicity/release/2007/200705/070530.html",children:"first dual-rotor car"}),", the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/en/publicity/release/2023/202310/231030b.html",children:"RX-7 followed in March 1978 and the RX-8 in April 2003"}),", and a rotary won ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://uk.mazda-press.com/news/2020/mazda-motor-corporation-1920-2020----a-century-of-defying-convention/",children:"Le Mans in 1991"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The RX-8's engine shows the scale of a production rotary: ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/en/publicity/release/2003/200301/0129e.html",children:"two rotors of 654 cc"}),", rated in its Type S form at ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"http://www.jsae.or.jp/autotech/category9/9-17/",children:"184 kW at 8,500 rpm and 216 N·m at 5,500 rpm"}),". RX-8 production ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/en/publicity/release/2023/202310/231030b.html",children:"ended in June 2012"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The newest Mazda rotary, the 8C, is an ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://uk.mazda-press.com/news/2023/mazda-mx-30-e-skyactiv-r-ev-revealed-at-brussels-motor-show-----------------------------uk-pricing-and-specification-announced/",children:"830 cc single rotor with a 120 mm radius and 76 mm width"}),". It generates electricity in the MX-30 R-EV with no mechanical connection to the wheels, and Mazda rates it at ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/ja/publicity/release/2023/202309/230914b.html",children:"53 kW and 112 N·m at 4,500 rpm"}),". Its market position has since shrunk: electrive reported in January 2026 that the car was ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.electrive.com/2026/01/14/mazda-discontinues-the-mx-30-r-ev-from-the-market",children:"being phased out in Europe, with Japanese production temporarily suspended"}),". Mazda's ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://newsroom.mazda.com/en/publicity/release/2025/202510/251029b.html",children:"Vision X-Coupe"})," carries a two-rotor rotary turbo, as a concept."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"How the Three Chambers Share One Rotor"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Follow one rotor face through one rotor revolution, the span the diagram below covers. The shaft turns three times while that happens, so a single face needs 1,080° of shaft to repeat its cycle, and each of the four processes gets 270°. A piston stroke gets 180° of crank. The torque measurements later in this article point to that extra 90°."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/rotary-chamber-cycle.svg",alt:"Diagram of the Wankel cycle: a triangular rotor inside a two-lobe epitrochoid housing shown at four positions through 1080 degrees of shaft rotation, with one chamber highlighted in turn as intake, compression, combustion and exhaust, plus the swept-volume formula"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.em,{children:"Drawn from the same epitrochoid and rotor-flank functions the simulator uses, at the 13B's dimensions."})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Three faces run that cycle a third of a rotor turn apart, which is why every shaft turn brings one firing per rotor. On the two-rotor preset, each chamber swings between 727.5 and 72.7 cc in the model, a ratio of 10.00."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The simulator is built on the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://patents.google.com/patent/US8720898B2/en",children:"3:1 shaft-to-rotor relationship"}),". The ratio is a definition in the model, so the count below confirms its bookkeeping and cannot test the ratio itself. We counted fire events over 90 shaft turns at a held 4,000 rpm, wide open:"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Rotors"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Fire events in 90 shaft turns"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Per shaft turn"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Spacing between firings"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"90"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.000"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"360°"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"180"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.000"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"180°"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"270"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.000"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"120°"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"4"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"360"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"4.000"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"90°"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That pulse rate explains the confusing displacement figures. Two rotors of 654 cc give 1.3 L of geometry, and a ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://jalopnik.com/2070903/rotary-displacement-vs-piston-engine-math/",children:"Jalopnik breakdown of rotary displacement"})," gives 2.6 L as the four-stroke equivalent, because a two-rotor sweeps its 1.3 L every shaft turn where a four-stroke sweeps its displacement every two. The pulse rate also picks the fair opponent: a two-rotor against an inline-four, and a three-rotor against an inline-six. The order those pulses arrive in is the subject of our guide to ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/firing-order",children:"firing order"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Rotary Engine vs Piston Engine"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A rotary makes more power per nominal litre and less pressure per unit of swept volume. These are the model's figures, with engines paired by pulses per shaft turn. BMEP, the mean effective pressure, uses T·2π/Vd for the rotary and T·4π/Vd for the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-four-stroke-engine-works",children:"four-stroke"}),", and peak rpm is approximate because the curves are flat there."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Engine"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Displacement"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak power"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak torque"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"BMEP"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"hp per nominal litre"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2-rotor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1,309 cc"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"159.1 hp at about 6,917 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"193.1 N·m at about 4,067 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"9.26 bar"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"121.5"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.0 L inline-four"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1,998 cc"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"116.4 hp at about 7,200 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"179.4 N·m at about 4,025 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"11.28 bar"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"58.3"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3-rotor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1,964 cc"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"237.6 hp at about 6,817 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"289.3 N·m at about 4,154 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"9.25 bar"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"120.9"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.0 L inline-six"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2,993 cc"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"193.5 hp at about 5,200 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"280.1 N·m at about 4,375 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"11.76 bar"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"64.7"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Most of the per-litre gap is the pulse rate. Rate the two-rotor at doubled displacement and its 159.1 hp over 2.62 L is 60.7 hp per litre, inside the range of the piston engines at 58.3 to 64.8. At near-equal nominal displacement the three-rotor makes 289.3 N·m against the four's 179.4, which is 61% more, and it does so from the same swept volume used more often."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Torque over one shaft turn"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The rotary's measured edge shows up inside a single turn. At 4,000 rpm and wide open, the two engines deliver almost the same mean torque from the same two pulses, and the shapes differ sharply."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/rotary-torque-vs-four-cylinder.svg",alt:"Line chart of brake torque over one shaft turn at 4,000 rpm. The 1.3 litre two-rotor rises from 18 newton-metres to two peaks of 445 and never crosses zero, with a mean of 192.5. The 2.0 litre inline-four rises to two peaks of 675 and falls to minus 153 between them, with a mean of 180.0. Peak torque over mean is 2.31 against 3.75, and flywheel energy swing is 205 joules against 362."})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Engine at 4,000 rpm, wide open"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Pulses per turn"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Mean N·m"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak N·m"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Lowest N·m"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak ÷ mean"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Degrees below zero"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Flywheel swing J"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2-rotor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"192.5"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"445"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"18"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.31"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"205"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.0 L inline-four"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"180.0"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"675"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"−153"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.75"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"34%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"362"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3-rotor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"288.3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"469"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"143"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.63"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"109"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.0 L inline-six"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"279.9"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"696"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"−29"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.49"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"15%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"230"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"5.0 L V8"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"4"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"444.7"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"832"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"137"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.87"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"174"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Flywheel swing is the energy a flywheel has to absorb and give back over the cycle: the two-rotor asks for 0.57 of the four's. A rotary reaches a piston engine's peak-to-mean with one pulse fewer per turn: the two-rotor's 2.31 sits with the sixes at 2.42 to 2.49, and the three-rotor's 1.63 beats the V8's 1.87."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is it the burn or the geometry?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The rotary burns over 75° of shaft from 15° before top dead centre, and the four over 60° from 12° before, so a softer burn could have explained the whole result. We swapped the burns both ways:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Arm, 4,000 rpm wide open"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Mean N·m"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak ÷ mean"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Degrees below zero"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak chamber bar"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2-rotor, stock burn"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"192.5"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.31"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"48.3"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2-rotor on the four's burn"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"197.1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.35"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"55.2"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.0 L four, stock burn"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"180.0"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.75"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"34%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"47.3"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.0 L four on the rotary's burn"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"163.5"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.62"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"34%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"39.0"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The burn moves peak ÷ mean by 0.04 on the rotary and 0.13 on the four, a fraction of the gap between the engines. Stock peak chamber pressures are level at 48.3 and 47.3 bar, so the rotary's torque smoothness has nothing to do with burning gently. The same pair throttled to about 95 N·m at 3,000 rpm keeps the order, 2.61 against 4.18."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"What remains points to stroke length. When the next rotor nears peak compression, the last still has 90° of expansion left. A four's expansion ends as the next cylinder reaches top of compression, and its torque spends a third of the cycle below zero."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"All of this is torque. Neither family in the model carries reciprocating inertia torque, so the piston numbers leave out the very thing a rotor lacks, and the simulator has no vibration or balance output. These figures say nothing about how either engine shakes."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Fuel, in the model and in the world"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Real rotaries are the thirstier engines. The EPA comparison in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/rotary-engine-comeback#why-it-died",children:"why the rotary died the first time"})," has the RX-8 using more fuel than a 3.5-litre V6. A ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://saemobilus.sae.org/papers/investigation-surface-area-volume-ratio-wankel-rotary-engines-affects-heat-transfer-heat-losses-2025-01-8357",children:"2025 SAE study"})," investigates how the chamber's surface-to-volume ratio feeds heat transfer and losses, though we would not hang the whole efficiency gap on that one ratio."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The model leans the same way at part load, and these figures are the model's only. At the same output and 3,000 rpm, the two-rotor's specific fuel consumption reads 3.6% above the four's at 30 kW and 10% above at 15 kW. It idles on 1.59 L/h against 0.75 L/h, which is 2.1×. Read those for direction only: the two families' combustion models differ, and neither sees the crescent chamber's quench or unburned fuel."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"How Porting Changes a Rotary Engine"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Ports are a rotary's valve timing, and in the model their placement changes the idle far more than the power. The preset's side ports, in the end plates, barely overlap intake with exhaust. A peripheral port, in the rotor housing, overlaps them for a long stretch. A ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://patents.google.com/patent/US3991722A/en",children:"Kawasaki patent"})," describes the same trade: side ports for low-speed stability, peripheral ports for high power at high speed, with exhaust overlap at low speed as the cost. Japan's mechanical engineering society records ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://jsme.or.jp/archive/eng/awards/awardsn03-5.html",children:"combustion stability at idle"})," as a rotary issue in its own right."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/rotary-idle-by-port.svg",alt:"Stacked bar chart of how burns split at idle on the same 2-rotor engine. The side port is 100 percent complete burns at 900 rpm and 1.6 litres per hour. The street port is 95 percent complete at 900 rpm and 1.7 litres per hour. The bridge port is 37 percent complete, 31 percent partial and 31 percent misfire at 1,200 rpm and 3.8 litres per hour. The peripheral port is 41 percent complete, 23 percent partial and 35 percent misfire at 2,400 rpm and 8.3 litres per hour."})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The model's numbers on the same two-rotor follow. Idle rpm is a set point the port pick writes, taken from published idles; the burn split, residual, scatter and fuel are measured at it. Peak power here is the on-screen dyno's reading, so the side port shows 159.2 hp where the table above prints 159.1."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Port"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Overlap"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Idle rpm"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Idle rpm range"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Complete / partial / misfire"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Residual gas"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Idle L/h"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak hp"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"N·m at 2,000 rpm"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Side"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"16°"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"900"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"886–913"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"100% / 0% / 0%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"29%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.59"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"159.2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"173.3"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Street"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"23°"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"900"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"842–941"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"95% / 4% / 1%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"35%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.73"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"160.2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"168.1"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Bridge"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"172°"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1,198"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1,054–1,332"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"37% / 31% / 31%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"43%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3.82"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"162.6"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"160.9"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Peripheral"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"151°"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2,400"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2,278–2,504"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"41% / 23% / 35%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"44%"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"8.32"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"162.9"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"157.2"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"From side to peripheral, at idle set points of 900 and 2,400 rpm, complete burns fall from 100% to 41%, and torque at 2,000 rpm drops 9.3%. Torque at 7,000 rpm rises 2.3%, and so does peak power. Idle fuel multiplies by 5.2, of which 2.67× follows from the higher set point; per shaft turn the fuel is 1.96×, which follows from the higher idle MAP set point the pick also writes."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Treat that 2.3% as a limit of the model. All four options share the same port areas; the pick moves the angles and writes the idle target and MAP set point. Rotary residual gas does not displace charge at full throttle, so the power gain is timing alone with no bigger hole. It says nothing about what porting buys on a real engine. We found no primary source for bridge port figures, so that row is the model's result alone."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why Do Rotary Engines Burn Oil and Wear Apex Seals"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Everything in this section comes from published sources, because the simulator models none of it. Its seals are a fixed leakage area with no wear, chatter or failure, and it has no oil, no emissions and no quench."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["An apex seal sits at each rotor tip and slides along the housing wall. That wall is also the wall the flame crosses, so whatever lubricates the seal shares the chamber with the charge. That path is our reading of the geometry; the sources confirm the outcomes. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://saemobilus.sae.org/papers/modeling-rotary-engine-apex-seal-lubrication-2015-01-2035",children:"SAE's modelling of apex seal lubrication"})," treats wear of the seal's running face as the problem to predict, and MIT's account of later rotary research names ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://news.mit.edu/2014/liquidpiston-small-efficient-rotary-engine-1205",children:"increased emissions and oil consumption"})," among the costs of the classic layout."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The same MIT piece describes the combustion chamber as long and thin, like a crescent moon. That shape feeds the emissions side. ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://trid.trb.org/View/217351",children:"SAE paper 780419, reported through TRID"}),", attributes 22% of hydrocarbon emissions to apex seal leakage and also points to quench at the chamber surfaces, where the wall cools the mixture below the point it will burn. For the history of seal failures and Mazda's fixes, see ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/rotary-engine-comeback#why-it-died",children:"the seal section of the comeback story"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Try It in the Simulator"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Open the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_2r_1L3",children:"2-rotor 1.3 L"})," and press START. The spec panel's Working chambers row reads 6 faces. Set TIME SCALE to 0.1× to watch the rotor walk round its shaft, then press RUN DYNO and compare the curve with the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=i4_2L",children:"2.0 L inline-four"}),". The on-screen dyno shows about 159.2 hp against 116.4 hp. The ripple figures came from a headless probe, so the dyno will not show them."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Step up the pulses with the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_1r_654",children:"1-rotor"})," and the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_3r_2L0",children:"3-rotor"}),", and listen to the note change at each firing rate. The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_4r_2L6",children:"4-rotor"})," has the pulse count of the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=v8_5L",children:"5.0 L V8"}),", but it is calibrated to a Le Mans race engine and idles at 2,400 rpm, so keep it out of per-litre comparisons."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The port links below carry an edited spec:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_2r_1L3&mod=eyJ2IjoxLCJwb3J0VHlwZSI6InN0cmVldCIsImludGFrZVBvcnRPcGVuRGVnIjoyNSwiaW50YWtlUG9ydENsb3NlRGVnIjozMzAsImV4aGF1c3RQb3J0T3BlbkRlZyI6NzI2LCJleGhhdXN0UG9ydENsb3NlRGVnIjo0OCwiaWRsZU1hcEJhciI6MC4zN30",children:"Street port on the 2-rotor"})," (needs a free sign-in): the idle starts to lope at 900 rpm."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_2r_1L3&mod=eyJ2IjoxLCJwb3J0VHlwZSI6ImJyaWRnZSIsImlkbGVScG0iOjEyMDAsImludGFrZVBvcnRPcGVuRGVnIjo5NjUsImludGFrZVBvcnRDbG9zZURlZyI6MzQyLCJleGhhdXN0UG9ydE9wZW5EZWciOjcyMiwiZXhoYXVzdFBvcnRDbG9zZURlZyI6NTcsImlkbGVNYXBCYXIiOjAuNjJ9",children:"Bridge port on the 2-rotor"})," (needs a free sign-in): a lumpy idle near 1,200 rpm."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=rotary_2r_1L3&mod=eyJ2IjoxLCJwb3J0VHlwZSI6InBlcmlwaGVyYWwiLCJpZGxlUnBtIjoyNDAwLCJpbnRha2VQb3J0T3BlbkRlZyI6OTk0LCJpbnRha2VQb3J0Q2xvc2VEZWciOjM0NSwiZXhoYXVzdFBvcnRPcGVuRGVnIjo3MzcsImV4aGF1c3RQb3J0Q2xvc2VEZWciOjY1LCJpZGxlTWFwQmFyIjowLjY4fQ",children:"Peripheral port on the 2-rotor"})," (needs a free sign-in): the port pick sets a 2,400 rpm idle."]}),`
