`]})]})}function Ct(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(F,{...n})}):F(n)}const St=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:Ct,frontmatter:we},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),be={title:"How Much Horsepower Does One Point of Compression Add? We Measured It",date:"2026-09-10",excerpt:"Forums say 3–4% per point. We swept an LS7 and a 4.6 Modular from 8.5:1 to 13.5:1 with knock on and off, and found where the gain stops being free.",readingMinutes:22,author:"Engine Simulator Team",draft:!1,image:"/blog/compression-ratio-cover.jpg",imageAlt:"Two forged aluminium pistons on a scarred steel workbench, one standing with its domed, valve-relieved crown toward the camera and one lying on its side, a feeler gauge fanned open in front of them, and a cylinder head out of focus behind with one combustion chamber lit by a warm lamp against blue window light"};function N(n){const t={a:"a",blockquote:"blockquote",code:"code",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",li:"li",ol:"ol",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"How much horsepower does one point of compression add? Ask on a forum and the answer arrives before you finish typing: three to four per cent, fading past 12.5:1. Nobody in the thread has measured it, because measuring it on a real engine means a new set of pistons for every data point."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In our simulator it is a slider. Load the LS7 and it makes 501.54 hp at its stock 11.0:1. Drag the slider to 12.0:1 and it makes 521.57. Drag it to 13.0:1 and it makes 540.09. Same heads, same cam, same fuel, with knock switched off, which on this engine's 98 octane changes nothing: the knock index reads 0.0000 at all three. Now put the same engine on 91 octane, hold it wide open at 4,500 rpm and watch the torque instead: 656.3 N·m at 11.5:1, 654.6 at 12.0, 647.6 at 13.0, 643.9 at 13.5. The slider kept going. The engine stopped following it."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["In our simulator one point of compression is worth ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"about 5.6%"})," of peak power at 8.5:1 and ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"about 3.3%"})," at 12.5:1, with knock switched off. The gain shrinks every half-point, and the curve is the same on a 7.0 L pushrod V8 and a 4.6 L SOHC V8 to within 0.1 point."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Over the full sweep, 8.5:1 to 13.5:1, the LS7 went ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"443.24 → 548.86 hp (+23.8%)"})," and the 4.6 Modular ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"256.08 → 316.34 hp (+23.5%)"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["On pump fuel the gain ends early. Wide open at 4,500 rpm, torque on both engines peaks at ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"11.5:1 on 91 octane"})," and ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"12:1 on 93"}),", then falls as the knock derate takes back what compression gave."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Premium fuel adds nothing until the engine is knock-limited: the 98-octane column and the knock-free control are identical everywhere the knock index reads ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.0000"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["The same 4.6 at ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"8.0:1"}),", with the turbo the model will fit on 91 octane, makes ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"492.8 hp"}),", against 316.34 for the naturally aspirated engine at 13.5:1 with knock switched off. A turbo engine runs low compression because that is where the power is."]}),`
`]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"On this page:"})," ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#quick-answer-how-much-horsepower-does-one-point-of-compression-add",children:"Quick answer"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-compression-makes-power",children:"Why compression makes power"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-sweep-two-v8s-knock-off",children:"The sweep, knock off"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-sweep-again-knock-on",children:"The sweep, knock on"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-turbo-engines-run-lower-compression",children:"Why turbo engines run lower compression"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#what-the-folklore-gets-right-and-wrong",children:"The folklore"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#what-this-simulator-does-not-model",children:"What we do not model"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#try-it-in-the-simulator",children:"Try it in the simulator"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#frequently-asked-questions",children:"FAQ"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-these-numbers-were-made",children:"How these numbers were made"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: How Much Horsepower Does One Point of Compression Add"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Between about 5.6% and about 3.3% of peak power per point in our simulator, depending on where you start: the first point above 8.5:1 is worth the most, and every point after it is worth less. Measured with knock switched off, an LS7 gained 23.8% from 8.5:1 to 13.5:1 and a Ford 4.6 Modular gained 23.5%, which compounds to 4.37% and 4.32% per point. Both engines land on the same per-step curve, so the number is a property of the thermodynamics rather than of either engine."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"On pump fuel you do not get to keep all of it. Held wide open at 4,500 rpm, torque on both engines stops rising at 11.5:1 on 91 octane and at 12:1 on 93, and every half-point past that is a loss. Those crossover points belong to this model, with its spark timing held fixed. A real build that retards timing as compression rises will cross later."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why Compression Makes Power"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compression ratio is the cylinder's volume at bottom dead centre divided by its volume at top dead centre: swept volume plus clearance volume, over clearance volume (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://powersports.jepistons.com/blog/compression-ratio-theory-and-how-to-calculate-in-powersports",children:"JE Pistons, Compression ratio theory and how to calculate"}),", retrieved 2026-09-10). Raising it squeezes the same charge into a smaller space before ignition, so the burnt gas expands through a larger ratio on the way back down and gives up more of its heat as work. A higher ratio improves thermal efficiency, and in a spark-ignition engine the limit on it is knock (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compression_ratio",children:"Wikipedia, Compression ratio"}),", retrieved 2026-09-10)."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ideal Otto cycle puts a number on it. Its thermal efficiency is η = 1 − 1/r^(γ−1), where r is the compression ratio and γ the ratio of specific heats of the working gas (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_cycle",children:"Wikipedia, Otto cycle"}),', retrieved 2026-09-10). The exponent is smaller than one, so efficiency rises with r and rises by less each time: the curve flattens. The flattening is why "per point" is the wrong unit to expect a constant answer from, and why the forum figure can be right at one compression ratio and wrong at another. You can watch the pressure trace change as the ratio moves in ',e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/otto-cycle-explained",children:"the Otto cycle explained"}),", which has a live P-V diagram."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A real charge is not air, and its γ is lower than the air-standard 1.4. Heywood's fuel-air cycle analysis (Heywood, ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"Internal Combustion Engine Fundamentals"}),", McGraw-Hill, 1988, ch. 5) puts the efficiency of a real mixture below the ideal and still rising with compression. Our measured gain ought to sit near that curve."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Sweep: Two V8s, Knock Off"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Two engines, so the result is not one engine's quirk. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"v8_7L0"})," is our LS7, a 7.0 L pushrod V8 at 104.8 × 101.6 mm, stock 11.0:1 on 98 octane. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"sohc_v8_4L6"})," is our Ford Modular 4.6 2V, 90.2 × 90.0 mm, SOHC, stock 9.4:1 on 92. One oversquare big-bore, one undersquare. Stock, on 29-point wide-open sweeps, they make 501.54 hp at 5,875 rpm and 268.97 hp at 5,054."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Then compression from 8.5:1 to 13.5:1 in half-point steps, with knock switched off. Our knock model reads octane, so setting octane to 200 removes the derate without touching anything else. That control is mandatory here: every combustion lever in this simulator reads backwards through the knock derate otherwise, so the thermodynamic gain has to be read with knock out of the way, and the knock-on runs read against it. Spark stays at each preset's fixed value for every step."}),`
