`]})]})}function qt(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(N,{...n})}):N(n)}const Et=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:qt,frontmatter:be},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ye={title:"Inline-6 vs V6: We Built Both at 3.0 Litres and Measured Them",date:"2026-08-25",updated:"2026-08-26",excerpt:"Two three-litre sixes on identical physics, one inline and one 60° V. Peak power lands 1.1% apart, and with every other variable held equal the layout is worth 0.02 hp. What it does decide: balance, packaging and boost.",readingMinutes:12,author:"Engine Simulator Team",draft:!1,image:"/blog/inline-6-vs-v6-cover.jpg",imageAlt:"Two bare aluminium engine blocks on a steel bench: on the left a tall inline-six block standing on end with six cylinder bores in a single row, on the right a shorter V6 block lying on its side with three bores visible on the near bank"};function D(n){const t={a:"a",blockquote:"blockquote",code:"code",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",img:"img",li:"li",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Ask which is better, an inline-six or a V6, and you get the same two sentences back: the straight-six is smoother, the V6 fits in more cars. Both are true. Neither tells you whether picking one costs you anything on the dyno, which is what you wanted to know."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"So we built both and measured them the same way, on one 28-step sweep each. Two 3.0-litre sixes on the same simulated physics, sharing a combustion model, a friction figure and a volumetric efficiency curve; one inline, one a 60° V. Then we stripped away every difference except the bank angle."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/i6-vs-v6-hero.svg",alt:"Two cylinder layouts drawn at matched scale: on the left six bores in a single row labelled inline-six, on the right the same six bores split into two banks of three at a sixty degree included angle labelled V6"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Our 3.0 L inline-six measured ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"193.4 hp @ 5,279 rpm and 280.2 N·m @ 4,336 rpm"}),". Our 3.0 L 60° V6 measured ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"191.2 hp @ 5,107 rpm and 284.7 N·m @ 4,150 rpm"})," — 1.1% apart on power, 1.6% on torque, in opposite directions."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["That gap is ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"bore and stroke, not layout"}),". The inline engine is undersquare (84.0 × 90.0 mm), the V6 oversquare (89.0 × 80.0 mm)."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["With bore, stroke, compression, cam timing and friction held identical and ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"only the bank angle changed"}),", the two engines produced ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"194.11 hp and 194.13 hp"})," — a difference of ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.02 hp, or 0.01%"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Layout is not a power lever. It decides balance, block shape and turbo placement."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["The straight-six's balance advantage is real and specific — free forces sum to zero ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"until the sixth order"})," — but it is the one claim on this page our simulator cannot measure, and we say so rather than dress it up."]}),`
`]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"On this page:"})," ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#quick-answer-which-makes-more-power",children:"Quick answer"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-two-engines-we-measured",children:"The two engines"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#the-controlled-test-change-only-the-bank-angle",children:"The controlled test"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#balance-the-claim-we-cannot-measure",children:"Balance"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#firing-order-and-why-they-sound-different",children:"Firing order"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#packaging-the-trade-that-decides-it",children:"Packaging"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-the-straight-six-suits-a-single-turbo",children:"Turbocharging"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#why-the-inline-6-came-back",children:"The comeback"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#so-which-should-you-want",children:"Which should you want"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#frequently-asked-questions",children:"FAQ"})," · ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"#how-these-numbers-were-made",children:"How these numbers were made"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: Which Makes More Power?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Neither, at matched displacement. Our two three-litre sixes finished within 1.1% of each other on peak power. The small gap that does exist comes from bore and stroke, geometry that says nothing about whether the cylinders sit in one row or two."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Bank angle"})," is the included angle between the two cylinder banks of a V engine: 0° means the cylinders sit in one row and the engine is an inline. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Bore"})," is the cylinder's diameter and ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"stroke"})," is how far the piston travels, and their product with cylinder count sets displacement."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/i6-vs-v6-torque.svg",alt:"Torque curves of a 3.0 litre inline-six and a 3.0 litre 60 degree V6 measured on the simulator dyno. The two curves overlap across the whole rev range. The inline-six peaks at 280 newton metres at 4336 rpm, the V6 at 285 newton metres at 4150 rpm"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Inline-6 3.0"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"V6 3.0, 60°"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Bore × stroke"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"84.0 × 90.0 mm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"89.0 × 80.0 mm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Displacement"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.993 L"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.986 L"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Compression ratio"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"11.0:1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"11.2:1"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Redline"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"7,400 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"7,500 rpm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Firing order"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1-5-3-6-2-4"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1-4-2-5-3-6"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Peak power"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"193.4 hp @ 5,279 rpm"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"191.2 hp @ 5,107 rpm"})})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Peak torque"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"280.2 N·m @ 4,336 rpm"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"284.7 N·m @ 4,150 rpm"})})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Read the last two rows. The V6 makes more torque and makes it lower down. The inline takes peak power by two horsepower, 172 rpm further up. If the layout were driving this, one column would take both."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Two Engines We Measured"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["We did not build either engine for this article. Both ship as stock presets in the simulator (",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i6_3L"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"v6_3L"}),"), tuned separately against the kind of engine each represents, so the pair samples how builders lay these engines out. We measured them at 11.0:1 and 11.2:1 compression, exactly as they ship."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The inline-six is undersquare: a 90 mm stroke against an 84 mm bore. Classic straight-six recipe, and it lands close to a real one: BMW's B58 runs 82 mm × 94.6 mm at 11.0:1 with a 7,000 rpm redline (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMW_B58",children:"Wikipedia, BMW B58"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25). Our preset sits within a couple of millimetres on both axes and shares the compression ratio."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The V6 goes the other way: 89 mm bore, 80 mm stroke, oversquare, revving 100 rpm higher. A shorter stroke means ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/tools/bore-stroke-calculator",children:"a bigger bore for the same swept volume"}),", which means more valve area and less piston speed per rpm."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Those two choices separate the curves. The bank angle does not."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Controlled Test: Change Only the Bank Angle"}),`
`,`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"To find out how much of the gap belongs to the layout, we deleted every other difference. Same bore, same stroke, same compression ratio, redline, cam timing, friction and volumetric efficiency. One square 86.0 × 86.0 mm six at 11.0:1, built twice: once with a 0° bank angle, once at 60°."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/i6-vs-v6-matched.svg",alt:"Bar chart of the controlled experiment. With everything but bank angle held identical, the inline-six produced 194.11 horsepower and the 60 degree V6 produced 194.13 horsepower, a difference of 0.02 horsepower or 0.01 percent"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak power"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak torque"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Inline-6, 0° bank"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"194.11 hp @ 5,279 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"281.18 N·m @ 4,336 rpm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"V6, 60° bank"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"194.13 hp @ 5,279 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"281.19 N·m @ 4,336 rpm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Difference"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.02 hp (0.01%)"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.01 N·m"})})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Our finding:"})," "," with every other variable pinned, the bank angle is worth two hundredths of a horsepower. Both engines peak at the same rpm, to the sample."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The swap test says it from the other direction. Give the inline engine the V6's bore and stroke and it makes 194.0 hp. Give the V6 the inline engine's and it drops to 190.6 hp. The numbers travel with the geometry, straight across the layout boundary."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"One reading of this result cuts against us. A thermodynamic model holds no opinion about where a cylinder sits, so part of this null result describes the model rather than the engine. The next section is where we hand you the tools to judge that."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Balance: The Claim We Cannot Measure"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The straight-six's reputation rests on balance, and the cause is geometry. Wikipedia's straight-six article states it:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['"The primary balance is due to the front and rear trio of cylinders moving in pairs (albeit 360° out of phase), thus canceling out the rocking motion present in a straight-three engine." (',e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straight-six_engine",children:"Wikipedia, Straight-six engine"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25)"]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'"The secondary balance is due to the crank throws being arranged in three planes offset at 120°, resulting in the non-sinusoidal forces summing to zero for all free forces until the sixth order."'}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Free forces summing to zero until the sixth order beats "smoother", because you can check it. The same source notes that V6, straight-four and V8 engines all "experience significant secondary dynamic imbalance, resulting in engine vibration." ',e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Secondary imbalance"})," shakes the engine at twice crank speed. The piston covers more ground on the way down from top dead centre than on the way back up, and that asymmetry is what shakes it."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Our simulator does not model this."})," It turns cylinder pressure into crank torque and treats rotating mass as inertia. The counterweights you can see in the 3D view are there to look at; they cancel nothing. So the most-cited advantage of the inline-six sits in the blind spot of every figure on this page, all of which describe gas and friction. If you came to find out which engine shakes less, the answer is the inline-six, and it comes from the crank throws rather than from us."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That limitation frames the 0.02 hp result. Layout does nothing ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"thermodynamically"}),", which hands the entire difference to balance, packaging and plumbing. Those are the three you feel from the driver's seat."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Firing Order and Why They Sound Different"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Both engines fire six times per two crank revolutions, spaced every 120°. The difference is the route those pulses take out of the block."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The inline-six sends all six down one exhaust side, into one manifold, in the order 1-5-3-6-2-4. The V6 splits them into two banks of three, each with its own collector, firing 1-4-2-5-3-6, so each bank sees an event every 240° of crank rotation and the two banks interleave."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The difference sits in the exhaust gas dynamics, not the crank, and it explains why the two layouts keep distinct voices even at matched bore and stroke. Run the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=i6_3L",children:"inline-six"}),", then the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=v6_3L",children:"60° V6"}),", and switch between the two tabs. The simulator synthesises its audio from firing events, so you are hearing the pulse train the physics produced rather than a recording."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Packaging: The Trade That Decides It"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Six bores in a row make a long engine. The same six in two banks of three make a short, wide one. The whole trade sits there, and it belongs to the chassis more than to the engine."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A long engine has to sit longitudinally, which commits you to rear or all-wheel drive, a long bonnet and a front axle line that clears the crank nose. A V6 lies across a transverse front-drive bay, sits lower and leaves crush space. When volume cars moved to front-wheel drive, engineers picked the V6 because the inline-six would not fit."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Width is the bill for that. As emissions hardware crowds the bay, the V6 gives ground: "as emissions equipment becomes more important their width now starts to count against them for packaging" (',e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.hagerty.com/media/news/why-inline-6-is-making-a-comeback/",children:"Hagerty"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25)."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why the Straight-Six Suits a Single Turbo"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"One head, one exhaust side, one manifold, one turbo. The path from valve to turbine is short and the pulses arrive evenly spaced. A V6 either takes two smaller turbos, one per bank, or runs a crossover pipe to feed a single one, paying for the choice in pipe length, heat lost on the way to the turbine, and the cost of a second housing."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That plumbing is why so many of the tuning world's favourite blocks are straight-sixes, and it shows up when you boost one. Turbocharged, the same 3.0 inline in our roster measures ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"385.5 hp @ 7,000 rpm and 544.0 N·m @ 4,656 rpm"}),", close to double the naturally aspirated figures from a block of identical displacement. The details of how that happens are in our guide to ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-turbocharger-works",children:"how a turbocharger works"}),", including why a wastegate creeps and why compression ratio sets a hard ceiling on boost."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why the Inline-6 Came Back"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"For twenty years, outside BMW, the straight-six looked finished. Modular architecture brought it back: one cylinder size, one bore spacing, one combustion chamber, scaled to three, four or six cylinders down a shared production line."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Mercedes' M256 is a 2,999 cc straight-six making 230–320 kW (313–435 PS) and 450–520 N·m. It replaced the M276 V6 and is the company's first petrol straight-six since the M104 (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercedes-Benz_M256_engine",children:"Wikipedia, Mercedes-Benz M256"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25)."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Despite carrying a 48 V starter-generator, the M256 is lighter than the V6 it replaced (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.hagerty.com/media/news/why-inline-6-is-making-a-comeback/",children:"Hagerty"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25)."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["BMW's B58 now appears in 41 models, including the Toyota Supra, Morgan Plus Six and Ineos Grenadier, in outputs from 213 to 324 kW (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.bmwblog.com/2025/09/30/bmw-b58-engine-41-models-update/",children:"BMWBlog"}),", 2025-09-30)."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Mercedes framed the straight-six as the centre of its engine strategy rather than a niche revival, pairing it with 48 V electrification across the range (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.evo.co.uk/mercedes/s-class/18474/new-inline-six-cylinder-heads-mercedes-engine-strategy",children:"evo"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25)."]}),`
`]}),`
`,`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Peak power appears in none of those justifications. Manufacturers went back for refinement, manufacturing commonality, turbo packaging and weight, which is the list our measurement predicts. The layout was never going to sell them horsepower."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"So Which Should You Want?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Rear-drive, and you care how the engine feels at 2,000 rpm"})," → inline-six. The balance argument is real and it is the layout's genuine advantage."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Transverse bay, low bonnet line, compact car"})," → V6. Not a compromise on output, just a different box."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Planning a big single-turbo build"})," → inline-six, for the exhaust path more than anything else."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Choosing on the dyno sheet"})," → choose on something else. At matched displacement the two layouts are worth 0.01% to each other, and the bore and stroke you pick move the curve far further than the bank angle ever will."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Both engines sit in the simulator, along with the sliders that produced every number above. Change the bore and the torque peak moves. Change the bank angle and nothing happens. That second experiment takes fifteen seconds, and it carries the whole argument of this article. If you want to go further, the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/build",children:"build wizard"})," will lay out a six of your own bore and stroke."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=i6_3L",children:"Run the inline-six"})})," · ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=v6_3L",children:"Run the V6"})})," · ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/engines",children:"Browse the full roster"})})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Frequently Asked Questions"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is an inline-6 more powerful than a V6?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Not because of its layout. Measured at matched displacement with every other variable held equal, our two engines finished 0.02 hp apart. Real-world differences between specific engines come from bore and stroke, compression, cam timing and induction. The inline-six in our test makes 1.1% more peak power than the V6, and swapping their bore and stroke carries that advantage across with it."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why are inline-6 engines smoother than V6s?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Because of how the crank throws are arranged. In a straight-six the front and rear trios of cylinders move in pairs 360° out of phase, which cancels the rocking motion. The throws also sit in three planes offset at 120°, so free forces sum to zero until the sixth order (",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straight-six_engine",children:"Wikipedia, Straight-six engine"}),", retrieved 2026-08-25). A V6 has no equivalent cancellation and carries significant secondary imbalance."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why did manufacturers switch to V6s in the first place?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Packaging. A V6 is short enough to sit across a front-wheel-drive engine bay and low enough for a modern bonnet line. The straight-six's length forces a longitudinal installation, which the volume-car market abandoned."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Can you turbocharge an inline-six more easily?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Yes. That is one of the layout's real advantages. All six exhaust pulses leave through one manifold on one side of the engine, so a single turbo mounts close to the head with a short gas path. A V6 needs twins or a crossover pipe. Our turbocharged 3.0 inline measures 385.5 hp and 544.0 N·m against 193.4 hp naturally aspirated."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is a straight-six the same as an inline-six?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Yes. Both names describe the same layout, six cylinders in a single row on one crankshaft. "Straight-six" is more common in British usage, "inline-six" in American.'}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"How These Numbers Were Made"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Test setup."})," Every figure labelled measured here came off the simulator's dyno. We measured each engine over a wide-open-throttle sweep from idle to redline in 28 steps, with fuel metering unlimited and the rev limiter ignored so the sweep reads the engine instead of the limiter. The controlled pair ran the same methodology: 4 variables pinned across both builds, 1 changed. Sampling waits for manifold pressure to settle before it records a point. The step size scales with rpm to hold roughly 12° of crank rotation per substep. Coarser than that and the burn lands in a different substep as the phase precesses, which alone can move a peak-power reading by double figures."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The model runs cycle by cycle: Wiebe heat release on Otto-cycle thermodynamics, torque taken from cylinder pressure through the crank slider, manifolds handled as lumped filling-and-emptying volumes. It leaves out vibration, engine mounts and noise transmission into a cabin, and it stops at separate collectors rather than modelling bank-to-bank exhaust interference. Read the 0.02 hp result inside those walls. It describes gas exchange and friction, which is also the part of the question a real dyno answers."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Real-world figures for the B58, M256 and the inline-six revival carry inline citations and belong to their sources. Where a number is ours, the text says measured."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Every run above reproduces from the presets shipped in the simulator: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i6_3L"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"v6_3L"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"turbo_i6_3L"}),". Load any of them, open the dyno, and you should land on the same curve. If you do not, we want to know. The simulator's dyno runs every engine in the same fixed air, so its figures are already reference-day numbers. The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/tools/dyno-correction-calculator",children:"dyno correction calculator"})," explains what that means and how to compare one with a real dyno sheet."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"About this article"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Written by the Engine Simulator Team, who build and calibrate the physics engine behind these numbers. We publish the model's limits beside its results, because a simulated figure is worth nothing without them."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Editorial policy."})," Every measured figure in this post is regenerated from the shipped presets and fact-checked against the simulator's own peaks file before publication. When a reader shows us a number is wrong, we correct the post and say what changed. Found one? ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/feedback",children:"Send it to us"})," or ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/contact",children:"contact the team"}),". For the model's full scope and its known gaps, read ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/about",children:"how we build the simulator and what it gets wrong"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Want the same treatment applied to induction instead of layout? We ran a turbocharger and a supercharger on the same block in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/roots-vs-centrifugal-supercharger",children:"Roots vs centrifugal superchargers"}),". The cycle both of these engines run is broken down in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-four-stroke-engine-works",children:"how a four-stroke engine works"}),", and the cycle they do not run is in ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/two-stroke-vs-four-stroke",children:"two-stroke vs four-stroke"}),"."]})]})}function Wt(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(D,{...n})}):D(n)}const Lt=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:Wt,frontmatter:ye},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),ke={title:"Jet Engine vs Rocket Engine: Which Is More Powerful?",date:"2026-06-11",updated:"2026-08-22",excerpt:"Jets breathe air; rockets carry their own oxygen. That one difference decides thrust, efficiency, and top speed. The full comparison, with real numbers.",readingMinutes:8,draft:!1,image:"/blog/j58-afterburner.jpg",imageAlt:"J58 turbojet engine on a ground test stand at night, firing a long blue afterburner exhaust plume"};function B(n){const t={a:"a",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",img:"img",li:"li",ol:"ol",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.em,{children:"Above: the SR-71's J58 turbojet in full afterburner during NASA ground testing. (Photo: NASA)"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A jet engine and a rocket engine both work the same way at the highest level: burn fuel, throw hot gas backwards, get pushed forwards. Newton's third law does not care which machine is doing the throwing."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In practice the two engines could hardly be more different. One can run for fourteen hours and cross the Pacific on a tank of kerosene. The other burns through hundreds of tonnes of propellant in eight minutes and is the only machine humans have built that works in space."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'This post covers what separates them and which one deserves the word "powerful".'}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: Jet vs Rocket"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A jet engine breathes oxygen from the atmosphere, while a rocket engine carries its own oxidizer onboard. So a rocket works in space and produces roughly ten times more thrust, and a jet is roughly ten times more fuel-efficient."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"jet engine"})," is air-breathing: it pulls oxygen from the atmosphere, so it only has to carry fuel. That makes it efficient, but it stops working where the air runs out, and it cannot push past roughly Mach 3 without melting its own inlet."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"rocket engine"})," carries both fuel ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"and"})," oxidizer onboard. That makes it heavy and thirsty, but independent of the atmosphere: full thrust at sea level, in the stratosphere, or in hard vacuum."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Raw thrust: rockets win by an order of magnitude. Efficiency: jets win by an order of magnitude. They answer two different questions."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"How a Jet Engine Works"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A turbojet is a continuous-flow machine. Air never stops moving through it, and four things happen to that air in sequence:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Suck"}),": the inlet and compressor swallow air and squeeze it to 10–40 times atmospheric pressure."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Squeeze"}),": compression heats the air to several hundred degrees before any fuel is added."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Bang"}),": fuel sprays into the combustor and burns continuously, raising gas temperature past 1,200 K."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Blow"}),": hot gas expands through a turbine (which powers the compressor, so the engine feeds itself) and accelerates out the nozzle."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Thermodynamically this is the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Brayton cycle"}),". The feedback loop is the elegant part: the turbine extracts enough energy from the exhaust to spin the compressor that feeds it. Below a certain RPM that loop cannot sustain itself, which is ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-jet-engine-starts",children:"why a jet needs a starter and a controlled light-off sequence"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The numbers: the General Electric J85, the small turbojet you can start and fly in our ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator",children:"engine simulator"}),", produces around 2,500 lbf of thrust dry. At the other extreme, the GE9X turbofan on the Boeing 777X holds the world record at over 134,000 lbf."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"How a Rocket Engine Works"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A rocket engine skips the entire front half of the jet. No inlet, no compressor, no turbine in the gas path. Instead:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Pumps"})," (or tank pressure) force fuel and oxidizer into the combustion chamber at enormous pressure, around 300 bar in SpaceX's Raptor."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The propellants burn at 3,300–3,600 K, hotter than the melting point of the chamber walls. The walls survive because fuel circulates through them as coolant."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["The gas accelerates through a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"converging-diverging nozzle"}),", going supersonic at the throat and exiting at 2,500–4,500 m/s."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Because the rocket brings its own oxidizer, the combustion chamber never needs the atmosphere. That is the entire trick and the entire cost: oxidizer outweighs fuel about 2.5-to-1, so most of a rocket's launch mass is oxygen hauled along to burn."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/rs25-hot-fire.jpg",alt:"Four RS-25 rocket engines firing at the base of the SLS core stage on a test stand, producing a huge white steam cloud"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.em,{children:"All four RS-25 engines firing during NASA's SLS Green Run hot fire, roughly 1.6 million pounds of combined thrust. (Photo: NASA/Stennis)"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Key Differences Between Jet and Rocket Engines"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Where the Oxygen Comes From"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"This is the root difference; everything else follows from it. A jet gets the ~3.4 kg of oxygen it needs per kg of fuel for free from the atmosphere (delivered in roughly 15 kg of air). A rocket pays mass for every gram of oxidizer. Free oxygen is why an airliner can carry 300 passengers across an ocean; carried oxygen is why a Falcon 9 is 95% propellant by mass at liftoff."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Thrust and Thrust-to-Weight Ratio"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Jets move a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"large mass of air a little faster"}),". Rockets move a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"small mass of gas enormously faster"}),". The rocket approach scales to absurd thrust levels:"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A modern fighter jet engine manages a thrust-to-weight ratio around 8–10."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["SpaceX's Merlin 1D exceeds ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"180:1"}),", one of the highest of any machine built."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A single F-1 engine on the Saturn V produced 1.5 million lbf; all five together out-thrust every jet engine ever made, combined, many times over."}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Fuel Efficiency (Specific Impulse)"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Specific impulse (Isp) measures how many seconds one pound of propellant can produce one pound of thrust. Chemical rockets top out around 450 seconds (the hydrogen-burning RS-25). A turbojet, counting only the fuel it carries, reaches an ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"effective"})," Isp of 3,000+ seconds. That ~10x gap is the price of carrying your own oxygen."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Operating Envelope"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A jet's world ends where the air does. Practical ceilings sit around 15–20 km, and inlet heating caps speed near Mach 3 (the SR-71's J58s lived at that edge). A rocket has no such limits. It performs ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"better"})," in vacuum, where the nozzle can expand the exhaust further."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Throttle Response and Control"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Jets respond slowly: spooling a heavy compressor from idle to full power takes several seconds, and aggressive throttle movement risks ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/compressor-stall-vs-surge",children:"compressor stall or surge"})," and flameout. You can trigger both and watch the event log narrate them in our ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=jet_j85",children:"turbojet simulator"}),". Liquid rockets respond within milliseconds to valve changes, but many can only throttle within a limited band before combustion goes unstable."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Jet Engine vs Rocket Engine: Side by Side"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Jet engine"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Rocket engine"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Oxygen source"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Atmosphere (free)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Carried onboard (paid in mass)"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Typical thrust"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2,500–134,000 lbf"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"100,000–1,700,000 lbf per engine"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Thrust-to-weight"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~5–10 : 1"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~70–180 : 1"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Specific impulse"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"3,000+ s (fuel only)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"250–450 s"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Exhaust velocity"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~400–600 m/s (turbofan)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2,500–4,500 m/s"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Speed limit"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~Mach 3 (inlet heating)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"None (orbital velocity and beyond)"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Altitude limit"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~15–20 km"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"None; improves in vacuum"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Burn duration"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Hours"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Minutes"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Starting"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Spool-up via starter, self-sustains"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Ignition sequence, immediate full flow"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"So Which Is More Powerful?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Define "powerful" first.'}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Peak thrust:"})," rockets, no contest. A single Raptor produces more thrust than three record-holding GE9X turbofans."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Useful work per kilogram of propellant:"})," jets, by roughly a factor of ten. No rocket-powered airliner could close its business case; the oxidizer mass kills it."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A rocket is a sprinter that carries its own air supply. A jet is a marathon runner that breathes. Each is hopeless at the other's job, which is why spaceplane proposals keep combining ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"both"}),", and why hybrid designs like the SR-71's turbo-ramjet J58 remain some of the most studied engines ever flown."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"See a Jet Engine Run, From Spool-Up to Flameout"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Reading about compressor surge is one thing; watching compressor speed climb past light-off while EGT responds is another. Our free in-browser ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=jet_j85",children:"J85 simulator"})," includes a full ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/blog/how-a-jet-engine-starts",children:"jet-engine start sequence"})," (starter spool-up, light-off, self-sustain) plus an event log that explains each state change in plain language. Compare its response with the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=jet_j85_fadec",children:"FADEC retrofit"})," to see how fuel scheduling protects compressor margin."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Jet Engine vs Rocket Engine FAQs"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Can a jet engine work in space?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"No. A jet needs atmospheric oxygen for combustion and atmospheric mass to accelerate. Above roughly 20 km the air is too thin to sustain either, which is why spacecraft use rockets."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Why don't airplanes use rocket engines?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Mass and cost. A rocket-powered airliner would need to carry roughly 2.5 kg of oxidizer for every kg of fuel, leaving almost no payload capacity. Rocket-powered aircraft have existed (the Me 163, the X-15) but measured endurance in minutes."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"What about scramjets? Aren't they jets that go faster than Mach 3?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Yes. A scramjet keeps airflow supersonic through the combustor, dodging the inlet-heating limit; NASA's X-43A reached Mach 9.6. Scramjets only ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"start"})," working around Mach 4–5, so they need a rocket or turbojet to get up to speed first."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Is a turbofan the same as a turbojet?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A turbofan is a turbojet wrapped around a huge fan: the core drives the fan, and most thrust comes from cold bypass air. Airliner engines are efficient and (relatively) quiet for this reason, while pure turbojets like the J85 are louder, thirstier, and better suited to high speed."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Which engine is harder to start?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Hard in different ways. A turbojet needs a starter to spin the compressor to self-sustaining RPM before fuel can light; get it wrong and you get a hot start or hung start. A rocket needs millisecond-precise ignition timing; propellant pooling before ignition is how engines explode on the pad."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Related Reading"}),`
