`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Quick Answer: Two-Stroke vs Four-Stroke?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A two-stroke completes intake, compression, power and exhaust in one crank revolution; a four-stroke needs two. At equal displacement, a two-stroke fires twice as often and should — in the simplest telling — make roughly double the power. Measured here across three real presets, the actual ratio ranged from ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"1.89x to 2.35x"}),", and the shortfall is dominated by a short, port-gated compression stroke rather than by lost charge alone. The two-stroke's ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"quality"})," of work per cycle — measured directly as brake mean effective pressure — is nearly identical to the four-stroke's. It just gets to do that work twice as often."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Difference Between a Two-Stroke and a Four-Stroke Engine"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The difference between a two-stroke and a four-stroke engine is how many crank revolutions one combustion cycle takes, and everything else follows from that. A four-stroke spends one full revolution pumping: the piston draws in charge on the way down and pushes exhaust out on the way up, through poppet valves a camshaft opens and closes. A two-stroke has no pumping revolution. Ports cut into the cylinder wall open when the piston is near the bottom of its stroke, the fresh charge pushes the burnt gas out through them, and the engine fires again on the very next stroke."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Four things differ as a result:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Two-stroke"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Four-stroke"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fires every"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1 revolution"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2 revolutions"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Gas exchange"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Ports in the cylinder wall, opened by the piston"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Poppet valves, opened by a camshaft"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Fresh charge moved by"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Crankcase (loop-scavenged) or a blower (uniflow)"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"The piston's own intake stroke"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Lubrication"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Oil mixed into the fuel, on a crankcase-scavenged engine"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"A separate wet sump"})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The rest of this article measures what each of those costs and pays: the first row is why a two-stroke should make twice the power, the second is why it does not quite, the third is why a premix two-stroke burns more fuel, and the fourth is why it smokes."}),`
