`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The two-stroke versions burn ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"31% and 22%"}),' more fuel per unit of work than the identical cylinder as a four-stroke. That is the real cost the "smoky two-stroke" reputation is about, and it is a genuinely separate number from the power ratio above — a two-stroke can be nearly as efficient per stroke (the BMEP result) while still being thirstier per unit of ',e.jsx(t.em,{children:"work delivered"}),", because a meaningful fraction of every intake charge never gets the chance to burn at all."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"The Uniflow Diesel Exception"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Not every two-stroke pays that fuel penalty, and the reason is a genuinely different architecture. A ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"uniflow"})," two-stroke — the layout large marine and locomotive two-stroke diesels use, exhaust valves at the top of the cylinder, intake ports low down fed by a mechanical blower — injects its fuel ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"after"})," the ports have closed. Whatever charge short-circuits straight through during scavenging is plain air, not a fuel-air mixture. The blower spends real work moving that excess air, but none of it was ever going to burn."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Measured on the diesel six at 1,800 rpm, wide-open throttle:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Power"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Fuel consumed"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"BSFC"})})]})}),e.jsx(t.tbody,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"6,974 cc, uniflow two-stroke @ 1,800 rpm"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"163.4 kW"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"53.49 L/h"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"275 g/kWh"})})]})})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That figure sits in ordinary four-stroke diesel territory, not the elevated numbers the premix two-strokes above show. It is the mechanical reason the largest, most efficient piston engines ever built — ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.man-es.com/docs/default-source/document-sync/man-b-w-two-stroke-engines-eng.pdf",children:"giant uniflow marine two-stroke diesels, documented at 50–52.5% thermal efficiency"})," — are two-strokes rather than four-strokes: at that scale the twice-as-many-power-strokes advantage comes essentially for free, because there is no premixed fuel to lose out the port."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Port Timing Is the Powerband"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The angle at which the exhaust port opens is arguably the single most consequential number on a two-stroke's spec sheet, and it is directly editable in the simulator's build tools. A ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"low"}),' angle after top dead centre is a tall, early-opening "race" port: blowdown starts sooner, the trapped compression stroke is short. A ',e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"high"})," angle is a mild, late-opening port: a longer trapped stroke, more low-end torque, less top-end."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/blog/two-stroke-port-timing.svg",alt:"Line chart of trapped compression ratio against exhaust port opening angle from 80 to 130 degrees after top dead centre, on a 249 cc single. Trapped compression ratio rises from 5.62 at 80 degrees to 9.28 at 130 degrees. Each point is annotated with the engine speed of peak torque, which falls from 7,664 rpm at the tallest port to 6,853 rpm at the mildest."})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Port opens (° ATDC)"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Trapped CR"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak power"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Peak torque, and where"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"80"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"5.62"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"40.2 hp"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["36.8 N·m @ ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"7,664 rpm"})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"100"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"7.29"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"45.6 hp"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"41.1 N·m @ 6,691 rpm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"130"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"9.28"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"48.2 hp"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["43.4 N·m @ ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"6,853 rpm"})]})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Trapped compression ratio rises monotonically and predictably across the sweep. The rpm of peak torque moves too, walking the powerband up or down the range as port timing changes. That is the actual physical lever a two-stroke tuner pulls when they talk about "porting."'}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"One honest limitation."})," This simulator does not model a ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://magazine.cycleworld.com/article/1964/11/1/tuned-exhaust-for-the-two-stroke",children:"tuned expansion chamber"})," — the resonant exhaust pipe that a real racing two-stroke uses to reflect an escaping charge back into the cylinder before the port closes. Real two-stroke tuning trades a tall port's early blowdown against exactly that returned charge; without a pipe model, this sweep can only show the trapped-CR side of that trade, not the recovery a real pipe provides. The powerband-shifting result above is real and reproducible; the absolute power numbers at the tallest port angles likely understate what a properly piped engine would recover."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Why Two-Strokes Largely Disappeared from Roads"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"If a two-stroke can genuinely out-produce an equal-displacement four-stroke, why do so few road vehicles use one? Two separate reasons, both visible in the measurements above:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Emissions."})," A premix loop-scavenged two-stroke's short-circuited fuel goes straight out the tailpipe, unburned, and ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-40/chapter-I/subchapter-U/part-1054",children:"US federal regulation now controls exhaust and evaporative emissions from these small nonroad spark-ignition engines directly"}),", with a ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.epa.gov/regulations-emissions-vehicles-and-engines/final-rule-phase-2-emission-standards-new-nonroad-0",children:"second phase of standards estimated to cut combined hydrocarbon and NOx emissions from handheld two-stroke equipment by roughly 70% against the earlier Phase 1 rule"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Part-throttle behaviour."})," A crankcase-scavenged engine's throttle typically sits ahead of the crankcase. Closing it starves the whole scavenging process, rather than simply reducing airflow the way a four-stroke's throttle plate does. That is a large part of why two-strokes are peaky and unpleasant to ride gently — and it is the opposite of the wide-open-throttle condition every number on this page was measured at."]}),`
`]}),`
