`]})]})}function Xt(n={}){const{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?e.jsx(t,{...n,children:e.jsx(U,{...n})}):U(n)}const Ut=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:Xt,frontmatter:Se},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),qe={title:"What Is a Camshaft? What It Does and What a Bigger Cam Costs",date:"2026-11-05",excerpt:"A camshaft decides when the valves open and close, so when the engine breathes. How lobes, lift, duration and overlap work, and what a bigger cam costs.",readingMinutes:15,author:"Engine Simulator Team",draft:!1,image:"/blog/what-is-a-camshaft.png",imageAlt:"A polished steel camshaft lying on a dark background, its row of offset lobes catching the light along the length of the shaft"};function Q(n){const t={a:"a",blockquote:"blockquote",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",img:"img",li:"li",p:"p",strong:"strong",table:"table",tbody:"tbody",td:"td",th:"th",thead:"thead",tr:"tr",ul:"ul",...n.components};return e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A camshaft is the rotating shaft that opens an engine's valves. Lobes ground along its length push each valve open and let a spring close it, so the shape of the steel decides when the cylinders breathe in and when they breathe out. That fixed shape has to serve every rpm the engine will ever see, which makes choosing a cam a trade: a longer one tends to move torque up the rev range and give away the bottom end."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["You can see the trade in our simulator. Open the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"/simulator?engine=v8_5L",children:"stock 5.0 L V8"}),", press RUN DYNO, and you have the baseline for the curves below. This article covers what a camshaft is, what it does, how lobes, lift, duration and overlap work, and what a bigger cam does, with the model's duration sweep set beside a real cam test on a 408 cubic inch LS."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.blockquote,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Key Takeaways"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A camshaft is a shaft of lobes, driven from the crankshaft, that opens the valves in step with the pistons. On a four-stroke it turns once for every two crank turns."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Four terms describe a cam: lift (how far the valve opens), duration (how long it stays open), lobe separation (the angle between the intake and exhaust lobes) and overlap (how long both valves are open together)."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["In our model of a 5.0 L V8, going from the stock 260° cam to 300° moves peak torque from ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"3,758 to 5,278 rpm"}),". Torque at 2,500 rpm falls from ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"429.3 to 332.2 N·m"}),", and torque at 6,500 rpm rises from ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"313.3 to 373.4 N·m"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.onallcylinders.com/2021/05/24/stroker-ls-cam-swaps-when-is-bigger-not-better/",children:"real 408 cubic inch LS"})," moved the same way: its torque peak went from 3,900 to 5,400 rpm across four cams. Lift rose with duration in that test, and a ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.onallcylinders.com/2019/10/31/truck-cam-swap-testing-to-add-low-end-torque/",children:"5.3 L truck swap"})," gained low-end torque with a longer cam, so the trade is a tendency with exceptions."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Rough idle is a real-engine result: ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://www.chevyhardcore.com/tech-stories/engine/thumpin-power/",children:"vacuum fell from 9 to 5.5 inHg"})," across three cams on a 350. Our model has no lope. It shows only idle manifold pressure rising, from ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"0.188 to 0.234 bar"})," at 300°, the right direction and far too small."]}),`
`]}),`
